        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "After thinking about it, unfortunately I don't see a way to do this without breaking existing functionality. Besides \"allow user to think as long as they like\" reason, there is one more. Current design of having only maps for `a` and `i` doesn't align with descriptions of textobjects as there are no *actual* mappings for them. But having them that way allows for a more targeted approach of creating buffer-local textobjects by just creating `b:miniai_config`.\r\n\r\nAll in all, I don't think it will be implemented. That said, 'which-key.nvim' doesn't have to have actual mappings to show window with suggestions. [Here](https://github.com/LazyVim/LazyVim/blob/befa6c67a4387b0db4f8421d463f5d03f91dc829/lua/lazyvim/plugins/coding.lua#L176-L215) is what Folke has done for LazyVim.\r\n\r\nThanks for suggestion, though!",
            "created_at": "2023-02-23T15:53:36Z",
            "html_url": "https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/192#issuecomment-1442020792",
